      "id": 2,
      "claim": "If Block A were a good thermal conductor, heat would flow rapidly from the hot base to the top surface, causing the top surface temperature to rise significantly within several minutes.",
      "evidence": "Fourier's Law of Heat Conduction: High thermal conductivity (k) results in high heat flux (q = -k∇T). For typical block dimensions, good conductors (like metals) equilibrate quickly.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The observation is that the top surface of Block A does not feel hot to the finger after several minutes of contact.",
      "evidence": "Problem statement: 'She keeps her finger on the block A for several minutes, but block A still does not get hot.'",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The failure of the top surface to heat up contradicts the prediction for a good conductor, implying that the material resists the flow of thermal energy.",
      "evidence": "Logical contrapositive: If Good Conductor -> Hot Surface, then Not Hot Surface -> Not Good Conductor (Poor Conductor).",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "A material that resists the flow of thermal energy is defined as a poor conductor (or thermal insulator).",
      "evidence": "Standard definition of thermal conductivity properties in physics.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [4]
    }
  ],
